================================================================================
Analytics → Ranked chart: bucket the metric series on the warehouse (crash fix)
================================================================================

Context:
Opening Analytics on a large domain crashed the deployed app (ontobricks-10x,
2026-11-13 13:47Z): ``GET /dtwin/metrics/series`` pulled one row per scored
node into the app process (``SELECT … ORDER BY`` + ``fetchall``) and only then
LTTB-sampled it in Python, so memory and payload grew with the graph and the
proxy returned 502 before the process died. The distribution histograms were
already binned in SQL and are unchanged.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/graph_analysis/JobMetrics.py
   ``metric_series_query`` now ranks nodes with ``ROW_NUMBER``, splits ranks
   into ``METRIC_SERIES_BUCKETS`` (1,000) equal buckets and returns only each
   bucket's first and last node with ``node_rank`` / ``total_count`` — at most
   2,000 rows whatever the graph size. Rank is widened to BIGINT before the
   bucket multiply (Spark INT overflow). Removed the Python LTTB
   ``sample_metric_series`` and the ``METRIC_SERIES_SAMPLE_*`` constants.
2. src/back/core/graph_analysis/__init__.py
   Export ``METRIC_SERIES_BUCKETS``; drop the removed symbols.
3. src/back/objects/digitaltwin/TwinAnalytics.py
   ``load_graph_metric_series`` builds the payload from the bounded rows; total
   comes from ``total_count`` and ``sampled`` is ``rows < total``. Response
   contract unchanged for the front end.
4. tests/units/core/test_job_metrics.py
   Replace LTTB tests with SQLite-executed bucketing tests (whole series below
   bucket count, bounded + edge-preserving above it, BIGINT widening).
5. tests/units/api/test_dtwin_metric_series.py, tests/units/dtwin/test_v090_twin_analytics.py
   Fakes carry ``node_rank`` / ``total_count``; new test for ``sampled``/total
   when the warehouse returns fewer rows than nodes.
6. docs/user-guide.md
   Describe rank-bucketed series (≤ 2,000 points) instead of 5,000/2,000 LTTB.

